DESCRIPTION:
Rectangular masonry cooking platform along the southeast wall of the room overlies earlier first style decoration. Tile and plaster surface survives in places with possible low molded lip. In the south corner are the masonry and plaster supports for a grill. The fire pit is filled with debris.

Attributes: grill

Citations: ESCHEBACH 99-100;
PPM 329;
JASHEMSKI 1979 176-177

One enters the square dining hall from one of two accesses; from the southeast one enters from the atrium thru a large tall door over a white marble threshold with tracking on both sides for a door. Opposite the door on the northwest wall is a large window which looks onto the peristyle and garden. Access from the southwest thru a lower wider doorway adjoins the tablinum. The threshold from this access was once a large darker slab of marble from which a large portion is missing. The lintel seems to be modern reconstruction. The room itself is in poor condition. Plaster is evident on all walls. On the northwest and southeast walls, molded ornate cornices give evidence of a vaulted ceiling, three possibly four layers of plaster suggest redecoration or remodeling in antiquity. Traces of red, yellow and white paint are evident, and on the northwest and southeast walls are decorative panels which mirror each other in approximate location;
the figures on the southeast wall are indiscernible, but appears to be a man and woman and on the northwest wall a single male figure can be seen. The floor is deteriorating, but is constructed of white tesserae. On the northwest - northeast and southeast walls high above the cornice are beam holes for a second story
support. The is non extant.

This niche is on the northwest side of the vicolo di Narcisso, on the northwest side of doorway of 6.2.16.

Earlier sources indicate that this is pre-sullan and possibly disused at the time of the eruption. While this niche maybe pre-sullan, its well-maintained interior plaster indicates continued usage. There is a fissure vertically through the niche and wall below.

The niche's sides and back are plastered. The sill has possibly been raised once. It is possible that the final sill was of stone (small piece of stone in situ).

The height of the sill from the current sidewalk is 0.475m;
the height from the sidewalk to the top of the niche is 0.93m;
the maximum width of the niche is 0.295m;
the niche's depth is 0.13m.
